 in  terms of providing development and  political stability and reviving the  Indian economy I think they are going to  be judged on entirely different matrix  of performance outcomes then foreign  policy or what they do with Pakistan  alright let's look at the Munich  Security Conference where one of the  themes this time round is the concept of  West lessness a slow decline if you like  of the Western led order that has been  in place after the end of the Second  World War  now if that decline accelerates would  you say in Asia India comes in to fill  that gap or is it China  I don't think India is an All India is  dependent India's significance in the  future is dependent on either the decay  of the West or the rise of China I think  India as one-sixth of humanity will play  a significant role any which ways now to  just spin your question a little bit  further I would argue that this is a  good moment you know we are going  through a silly season across the world  we are having debates on citizenship on  borders on sovereignty on  multilateralism on populism and this is  these are secular debates happening  everywhere I think this is the time for  new powers to stake claim towards  leadership in fact unabashedly let me  put forward a new book that Jessie and I  have written dr. Shashi Tharoor and I  put forward as its title the New World  Disorder and the Indian imperative and  we argue that based on our largely  liberal traditions are largely  market-based economics are the plural  and argumentative democracy it is  perhaps time for new actors like India  to add their hef to the liberal order  which may not remain that liberal if  India was not to be a part of that and  it certainly is not going to be Western  in as the Asian century progresses now  that Western element will have to be  replaced by certain new Eastern liberal  ideas and India is best placed and best  suited to do that so I don't think it's  restlessness but it's going to be West  and the East 